From 7d63385451d64e88bbd13446d038a88a977a0c13 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 05:09:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: editions: no DNS before the dialog, no old
 messages while asking, an EDL cut kept

From a reading of the commits before.

- No name is resolved on the application thread before the busy
  dialog. URIUtils::IsLocalOrLAN() asks IsOnLAN() before it looks at the
  protocol, and for a server named with dots IsHostOnLAN() asks
  CDNSNameCache::Lookup(): with DNS down, seconds of the interface held,
  before any dialog. AskBeforePlayback() now looks at the protocol only
  (http/https are read by ranges; URIUtils::IsInternetStream(), which
  resolves nothing, are not read) and the reading of a file asks
  IsLocalOrLAN() on its own thread, under the busy dialog and its
  deadline.
- Nothing of the playback before is handled while the edition is asked.
  PlayFile() removes the delayed messages of the item before
  (GUI_MSG_PLAYBACK_ENDED, GUI_MSG_QUEUE_NEXT_ITEM and the others of
  previousMsgsIgnoredByNewPlaying) before it opens the new one; the
  question came first, and the loops of its dialogs (the busy dialog of
  the reading, the select dialog) dispatch thread messages, so a
  delayed PLAYBACK_ENDED could start the next item of a playlist, a
  PlayFile() inside the question. Now they are removed before the
  question, removed as they come while it is open (CApplication::
  Process(), the only place thread messages are dispatched, removes them
  first while g_askingBeforePlayback is above 0; every modal loop goes
  through it, CGUIWindowManager::ProcessRenderLoop()), and removed again
  before OpenFile(), as before. The new item starts right after the
  question in any case, and Kodi drops these messages when a new item
  starts. Messages of another kind (a TMSG of JSON-RPC that starts
  playback) are handled as in any modal dialog of Kodi's.
- A cut of an edit decision list file identical to a cut of the edition
  that plays is the file's. The file's cut is read first, CEdl::AddEdit()
  refuses the edition's (an edit is not applied twice), and the list
  holds it once. EditionSavedPosition() took every cut of the list equal
  to one of the edition's as the edition's, so for a position saved with
  another edition, or with none, that cut of the file was lost and the
  position placed early by its length. CEdl::AddCuts() now says which
  cuts it took, VideoPlayer keeps them (m_editionCutsInList), and only
  those are the edition's (MKV_EDITIONS::EditsOfFiles(), pure).
  AddEdit()'s rule is unchanged.

The first two run in Kodi only: the reasoning is above, and none of it
is run on a device. The third is in the host test (editions_fix_test):
with the classification of before the position is placed 5 s early, with
this one where it was saved; a mutation that takes every cut of the list
as the edition's makes it fail.
